\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{Keep the following separate.}
  \ul{
    \li{\code{LevelEq} compares fixed-label presentations by levelwise weak equivalences. It is the relation used for Reedy replacement and relative presheaf mapping invariance.}
    \li{\code{VDCEq} is the candidate categorical equivalence of augmented VDC presentations. Its provisional local specification requires equivalences on raw vertical hom spaces and on cell spaces over every fixed source boundary, and \em{simultaneous} essential surjectivity: choose object representatives with vertical equivalences, and for each target horizontal choose a source horizontal and an invertible unary cell over those chosen endpoint equivalences. Invertibility and compatibility are formulated through coherent unary-cell diagrams, not through the deleted \code{hP}.}
  }
  \p{There is no requirement that \code{P(h) → Q(h)} be a weak equivalence. In the discrete case the comparison target is Koudenburg's equivalence in \code{AugVirtDblCat} (with vertical isomorphisms). For Segal categories themselves use their standard DK-equivalence. The complete ∞-clause list and its relation to other VDC models remain \ref{ms-foundation} research obligations; the provisional predicate is not exported as a settled definition.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{Equivalent presentations may have different sets of isomorphic horizontals. Different endpoint representatives must work together for all cells.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rejected}
  \p{The original horizontal-object bijection condition; checking horizontals only at literal image endpoints; deriving the ∞-comparison from the discrete case alone.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Acceptance}
  \p{\ref{at-fd-0005}: duplicate horizontals, endpoint transport, and 2-out-of-3; \ref{at-rt-0005} in its revised scope. \ref{at-rt-0009} remains the full external comparison.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{provisional. \ref{ms-foundation} may change these local clauses if their tests fail.}
}
